```java
interface Shape {}
class Circle implements Shape {}
class Square implements Shape {}

class ShapeFactory {
    static Shape create(String type) {
        return switch (type) {
            case "circle" -> new Circle();
            case "square" -> new Square();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
        };
    }
}
```

Callers never touch `Circle` or `Square` directly — they ask the factory for a `Shape` and get whichever concrete type fits the input. Nothing here is about *assembling* a complex object; it's entirely about *which* simple one to hand back.

A well-written Builder defers validation to `build()`, not to each setter — so callers can set fields in any order without tripping premature errors:

```java
class RequestBuilder {
    private String method, url;
    RequestBuilder method(String m) { this.method = m; return this; }
    RequestBuilder url(String u) { this.url = u; return this; }
    HttpRequest build() {
        if (method == null || url == null)
            throw new IllegalStateException("method and url are required");
        return new HttpRequest(method, url);
    }
}
```

Throwing inside `url()` the moment `method` hasn't been set yet would force callers into one specific call order — the opposite of what a fluent builder is for.
